# aeneid-026 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Iarbas. Mercurius. Dido furens"
TITLE_EN = "Iarbas's Prayer, Mercury's Errand and the Queen's Fury — Aeneid IV.206–304"
PART = "Iarbas's Prayer, Mercury's Errand and the Queen's Fury (IV.206–304)"
ABOUT = ("Iarbas, the African king, finishes his prayer to Jupiter. Does the god see what is happening, he asks, or are his thunderbolts only empty noise? A woman who wandered into his country, bought a small city, and was given a shore to plough and the terms of the land, has refused marriage with him and taken Aeneas as her master: that second Paris, with his half-man attendants, his chin and scented hair bound up in a Lydian bonnet, enjoying what he has stolen, while Iarbas brings gifts to Jupiter's temples and cherishes an empty rumour. Jupiter hears him, turns his eyes to the royal city and the lovers who have forgotten their better name, and sends Mercury with a message: the Trojan leader is idling in Carthage and ignoring the cities that fate has given him. His mother never promised him to Jupiter as such a man; he was to rule an Italy heavy with empire, hand down a race from Teucer's high blood, and bring the whole world under law. If glory does not stir him, let him think of his son. Let him sail. Mercury binds on his golden winged sandals and takes his wand, with which he calls pale souls out of the underworld and gives and takes away sleep. Flying through the clouds he sights the peak of Atlas, his grandfather, with its pines, black clouds, snowy shoulders and icy beard, and he drops from there to the Libyan shore like a low-flying bird. He finds Aeneas founding citadels and building houses, a jasper-studded sword at his side and a purple cloak that Dido made hanging from his shoulders, and he rebukes him: forgetful of his kingdom, and a besotted husband building another's city, what is he doing in Libya? He repeats Jupiter's message, ending with the hopes of Ascanius, who is owed the kingdom of Italy and the land of Rome. Then, in the middle of his speech, he vanishes into thin air. Aeneas stands dumb with fear, his hair on end, his voice caught in his throat, and burns to flee. But how is he to approach the frantic queen? His mind turns this way and that, and at last he decides: he summons Mnestheus, Sergestus and Serestus, and tells them to fit out the fleet in silence and gather the men at the shore without saying why, while he looks for the gentlest moment to speak to Dido, who knows nothing and cannot imagine that so great a love could be broken. The men obey gladly. But the queen — who can deceive a woman in love? — senses it beforehand, and then the same wicked Rumour brings her word that the fleet is being armed. She rages through the whole city like a Bacchant roused by the sacred rites on night-time Cithaeron, and at last speaks to Aeneas first; her words come in the next part.")
U = [
(210, 'miscent?', "“Jupiter almighty, to whom now the Moorish, on embroidered\ncouches, people having feasted, pours the Lenaean tribute,\ndo you see these things, or at you, father, when you hurl the lightnings,\nin vain do we shudder, and blind fires in the clouds\nterrify our minds and stir up empty rumblings?", "“Almighty Jupiter, to whom the Moorish people now pour out their wine as they feast on embroidered couches, do you see this? Or do we shudder at you in vain, father, when you hurl your lightning, and are those blind fires in the clouds only a terror to our minds, an empty muttering of thunder?", "This opens the prayer of Iarbas, the African king introduced at the end of the last part, who is a son of Jupiter Hammon and so calls the god 'genitor'. It is a speech, so Greenough prints the opening quotation mark; it runs to line 218. 'cui… libat' — 'cui' is a dative, 'to whom'; 'epulata' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'epulor' with an active sense, 'having feasted'; 'pictis toris' is an ablative of place, 'on embroidered couches'. 'Lenaeum honorem' is wine as an offering: Lenaeus is a title of Bacchus, god of the wine-press. The Maurusii are the Moors of north-west Africa. 'aspicis haec, an… horremus' is a double question, 'do you see this, or do we shudder?', and the answer Iarbas fears is the second. 'caeci… ignes' are the lightning flashes, 'blind' because they seem to strike at random. 'inania murmura' is the thunder, 'empty rumblings'. Note that 'terrificant' and 'miscent' share the subject 'ignes'."),
(214, 'recepit.', "A woman, who wandering in our territories a city\nsmall for a price established, to whom a shore to plough\nand to whom the laws of the place we gave, our marriage\nhas rejected, and has received Aeneas into her realm as lord.", "A woman who wandered in our territory and set up a paltry city for a price (we gave her a shore to plough, and terms for the land) has spurned a marriage with us, and has taken Aeneas into her kingdom as its lord.", "'Femina' is contemptuous: Iarbas will not call her queen. 'errans' is a present participle agreeing with 'Femina'; 'exiguam' agrees with 'urbem'. 'pretio posuit' has an ablative of price, 'founded for a price' (the story is that Dido bought her site from the local people). 'litus arandum' is a gerundive in agreement with 'litus', 'a shore to be ploughed', and it is the object of 'dedimus', which 'cuique' ('et cui') shares with 'loci leges'. 'reppulit' is the perfect of 'repello' with the 'p' doubled for metre. 'conubia' is the neuter plural, 'marriage'. 'dominum' is a predicate accusative, 'as lord'. The main verbs, 'reppulit' and 'recepit', are held until the long relative clause is finished."),
(217, 'potitur:', "And now that Paris, with his half-man retinue,\nhis chin with a Maeonian cap and his dripping hair\nbound up, possesses the thing seized:", "And now that Paris, with his train of half-men, his chin and his scented hair bound up in a Lydian bonnet, possesses what he has stolen:", "Iarbas calls Aeneas 'ille Paris', the Trojan prince who carried off Helen: a thief of another man's woman, and, to Roman prejudice, an effeminate Easterner. 'semiviro comitatu' is an ablative of accompaniment, 'half-men' being a slur on the attendants. 'Maeonia… mitra' — Maeonia is Lydia in Asia Minor, and the 'mitra' is an Eastern headband or bonnet. 'subnexus' is a perfect participle with a middle sense, 'having bound up (his own)', and 'mentum… crinemque madentem' are retained accusatives (Greek-style): 'bound up as to chin and hair'. 'madentem' is 'dripping', i.e. with perfumed oil. 'rapto' is a perfect participle used as a noun, 'the thing seized', an ablative with 'potitur' (the deponent 'potior' governs the ablative). The 'plunder' is Dido and her kingdom."),
(218, 'inanem.”', "we gifts to the temples\nindeed of yours bring, and cherish an empty rumour.”", "while we carry gifts to your temples, to be sure, and nurse a hollow rumour.”", "'nos' is set against 'ille': Iarbas's own loyalty is shown as foolishness. 'quippe' is bitterly ironic, 'to be sure'. 'famam… inanem' is 'an empty report', the hollow fame of Jupiter's power; 'fovemus' means 'cherish, keep warm'. This prepares for Fama, who has just carried the story to him. The closing quotation mark is Greenough's."),
(221, 'amantes.', "Him praying with such words and holding the altars\nthe almighty heard, and turned his eyes to the walls\nroyal, and the lovers forgetful of their better fame.", "As he prayed in these words, clasping the altars, the almighty heard him, and turned his eyes to the royal city and the lovers who had forgotten their better name.", "'orantem… tenentem' are present participles in the accusative, agreeing with an unexpressed 'eum' (Iarbas), the object of 'audiit' (a shortened form of 'audivit'). 'omnipotens' stands alone for Jupiter. 'moenia' ends one line and its adjective 'regia' begins the next; the sentence closes on the lovers. 'oblitos' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'obliviscor', which takes the genitive ('famae melioris'): 'forgetful of a better reputation'. 'amantes' is a present participle used as a noun, 'lovers'. The Latin Library has 'amantis', which does not change the sense. This line begins a new paragraph in Greenough."),
(222, 'mandat:', "Then thus Mercury he addresses and such things commands:", "Then he spoke to Mercury, and gave him this charge:", "'adloquitur' is the present of the deponent 'adloquor', which takes the accusative ('Mercurium'). 'mandat' is 'entrusts, commits to', and 'talia' is its object. The god is named here by his Roman name; the speech of Jupiter runs to line 237. Mercury is the gods' messenger; he is also called 'Cyllenius' below, for Mount Cyllene in Arcadia, where he was born."),
(226, 'auras.', "“Go, come on, son, call the Zephyrs and glide on your wings,\nand the Dardan leader, who now in Tyrian Carthage\nwaits, and does not look back at the cities given by the fates,\naddress, and carry my words through the swift breezes.", "“Up, my son, and go: summon the Zephyrs, glide down on your wings, and speak to the Trojan captain who lingers in Tyrian Carthage and gives no thought to the cities that fate has granted him; carry my words through the swift air.", "'Vade age' is a brisk opening, 'go, come now', with 'age' reinforcing the imperative. 'labere' and 'adloquere' are singular imperatives of the deponents 'labor' ('glide') and 'adloquor' ('address'); the deponent imperative ends in '-ere'. 'defer' is the short imperative of 'defero'. The Zephyrs are the west winds, here the winds in general. 'Dardanium ducem' is the object of 'adloquere', which does not arrive until the start of 226; the relative clause 'qui nunc exspectat… non respicit' hangs between them. 'Tyria Karthagine' is an ablative of place, with no preposition. 'datas' agrees with 'urbes': the cities given (that is, destined) by fate, which means Italy. 'celeris' is the old accusative plural in '-is' (you'll see this constantly: 'pallentis', 'dulcis', 'partis')."),
(228, 'armis;', "Not him, to us, his mother most beautiful, such a one\npromised, and not for this twice from the arms of the Greeks does she rescue him;", "His mother, fairest of goddesses, did not promise him to me as such a man, nor was it for this that she twice rescued him from the arms of the Greeks;", "'genetrix pulcherrima' is Venus. 'talem' is a predicate accusative: 'promised him to be such a man'. 'nobis' is the plural of majesty, 'to me'. 'Graium… armis': 'Graium' is the old genitive plural in '-um' for 'Graiorum', so 'from the arms of the Greeks', with 'vindicat' (claim free, rescue) taking 'armis' as what he is rescued from. The 'non' of the first clause has to be carried over to the second ('and not for this reason does she…'): the Latin repeats no negative, but the sense demands it, since Jupiter's complaint is that the rescues were for another purpose than this idleness. 'bis' ('twice') counts two rescues without naming them, and 'vindicat' is a present of continuing action. The speech uses Jupiter's own grievance: Venus's promise of Aeneas was a promise of a founder."),
(231, 'orbem.', "but that there would be one, who the pregnant with empires and with war roaring\nItaly would rule, a race from the lofty blood of Teucer\nwould hand down, and the whole world under laws would send.", "but that he would be the man to rule an Italy heavy with empires to come and loud with war, to hand down a race from the high blood of Teucer, and to send the whole world under law.", "'sed fore' is 'but that there would be', with 'fore' the future infinitive of 'sum' ('futurum esse'), after 'promisit'; the Latin does not name the man. 'qui… regeret… proderet… mitteret' is a relative clause of purpose or characteristic with three subjunctives, 'one who should rule, bring forth, send'. 'gravidam imperiis belloque frementem' are both accusative adjectives agreeing with 'Italiam', which is held over to the start of the next line: 'pregnant with empires and roaring with war'. 'alto a sanguine Teucri' has a preposition set between adjective and noun; Teucer was the old ancestor of the Trojans (hence 'Teucri' for Trojans). 'proderet' here means 'hand down, transmit'. 'leges' are the laws of Rome. This is what Jupiter promised in Book I."),
(234, 'arces?', "If no glory of such great things fires him,\nnor himself, over his own praise, does he work at the toil,\nto Ascanius the father begrudges the Roman citadels?", "If the glory of such great enterprises does not fire him, and he does not himself undertake the toil for his own renown, does the father begrudge Ascanius the citadels of Rome?", "'Si nulla accendit' has an elision, 'nulla accendit' for the metre (you'll see this constantly). 'tantarum… rerum' is a genitive plural with 'gloria', 'of so great affairs'. 'super… sua laude' — 'super' with the ablative means 'about, for the sake of', so 'for the sake of his own praise'. 'molitur laborem' is 'sets about the toil'. 'Ascanione' is the dative of Ascanius with the question particle '-ne' ('Ascanio' + 'ne'), and the dative goes with 'invidet', which takes the dative: 'begrudge Ascanius'. 'pater' is Aeneas. This sentence is a protasis ('si') followed by a question; Mercury will repeat it almost word for word at 272–273."),
(236, 'arva?', "What does he build, or with what hope among a hostile people does he linger,\nand does not regard the Ausonian offspring and the Lavinian fields?", "What is he about, or with what hope does he linger among a hostile people, and give no thought to his Italian offspring or the fields of Lavinium?", "'Quid struit' is 'what is he building (contriving)?'; 'qua spe' is an ablative of attendant circumstance, and 'inimica in gente' puts the preposition between adjective and noun, as at 230. The 'hostile people' are the Carthaginians. 'nec' continues the question: 'and not regard?' 'Ausoniam' is 'Ausonian', an old name for Italy. 'Lavinia… arva' are the fields round Lavinium, the city Aeneas is fated to found in Italy; it is named after Lavinia, who is yet to be introduced. 'respicit' means 'give a thought to, look back to'."),
(237, 'summa est;', "Let him sail: this is the sum;", "Let him sail: that is all of it;", "'Naviget' is a present jussive subjunctive, 'let him sail'. 'summa' is the feminine noun 'the sum, the sum of the matter', and 'haec' is the subject. The speech is terse where the questions before it were long."),
(237, 'esto.”', "this of us the message let it be.”", "let that be the word I send.”", "'hic' is the pronoun 'this', pointing back at 'haec summa'. 'nostri' is the genitive of 'nos' standing for 'mei' ('of me'): Jupiter speaks of himself in the plural. 'nuntius' ordinarily means 'messenger'; here 'message' is the sense wanted, and Lewis & Short record that use. The line could also be heard as 'let this one be my messenger', if 'hic' points at Mercury; we follow 'message'. 'esto' is the future (second) imperative, 'let it be'. The closing quotation mark ends Jupiter's speech (223–237) and is Greenough's."),
(238, 'Dixerat.', "He had spoken.", "He had spoken.", "'Dixerat' is the pluperfect, the usual way to close a speech. This begins a paragraph."),
(239, 'imperio;', "He, of the great father to obey was preparing\nthe command;", "He made ready to obey the command of his mighty father;", "'Ille' is Mercury. 'patris magni… imperio': 'parere' (the infinitive of 'pareo', 'obey', and not of 'pario') takes the dative 'imperio', which is held over to the next line. The father is Jupiter. 'parabat' is an imperfect, 'was getting ready'."),
(241, 'portant;', "and first to his feet the winged sandals he binds\ngolden, which him on high on wings, whether over the seas\nor over the land, with swift breath alike carry;", "and first he fastens to his feet the golden winged sandals, which carry him high on their wings over water or over land alike, on a rushing blast of wind;", "'talaria' are winged sandals (from 'talus', ankle), the traditional footwear of Mercury; the word is a neuter plural, the object of 'nectit' and the antecedent of 'quae… portant'. 'aurea' is delayed to the start of the next line. 'sublimem' agrees with an unexpressed 'eum' (Mercury), 'carry him aloft'. 'sive aequora supra seu terram' has 'supra' placed after its noun (anastrophe) and serving both objects. 'rapido cum flamine' is an ablative of manner, 'with a swift blast'. 'pariter' means 'equally, alike'."),
(242, 'capit:', "then the wand he takes:", "then he takes his wand:", "'virga' is the wand of Mercury, usually called the caduceus; Virgil here uses the plain word. The colon introduces what it can do."),
(244, 'resignat.', "with this he calls out souls from Orcus\npale, others under gloomy Tartarus he sends,\ngives sleep and takes it away, and the eyes, from death, he unseals.", "With it he calls out pale souls from Orcus, sends others down to grim Tartarus, gives sleep and takes it away, and opens eyes that death has sealed.", "Mercury is the guide of souls ('psychopompos'). 'hac' is the ablative of means, 'with this'. 'Orco' is an ablative of separation with 'evocat'; 'Orcus' is the world of the dead. 'pallentis' is the accusative plural in '-is', agreeing with 'animas', and 'alias' (sc. 'animas') is set against it, 'others'. 'sub Tartara tristia' has 'sub' with the accusative of motion toward; 'Tartara' is a neuter plural, the deepest part of the underworld. 'dat somnos adimitque' — 'adimit' has 'somnos' as an understood object. 'resignat' is the hard word. 'signare' is 'to seal', so 'resignare' is basically 'to unseal, open' (a sealed letter or tablet), and 'lumina morte resignat' is then 'he opens eyes that death had sealed', which is how we render it. But 'resignare' also means 'to cancel, annul', and 'morte' can be taken as 'by death' or 'from death', so the clause can be read in more than one way: 'opens the eyes that death has sealed', 'cancels death for the eyes', or even 'seals the eyes in death'. We take the first, the plain meaning of the verb; the reading is not settled. The line is compact, a list of what the wand does."),
(246, 'nubila;', "Relying on that he drives the winds, and swirling\nclouds he swims across;", "Trusting in it, he drives the winds before him and swims across the stormy clouds;", "'Illa' is the wand (feminine ablative), the ablative which 'fretus' governs; 'fretus' is an adjective meaning 'relying on'. 'agit ventos' means that he drives the winds before him. 'turbida' agrees with 'nubila', a neuter plural noun ('clouds'), here 'swirling, troubled'. 'tranat' is 'swims across', the verb of a swimmer, used of the god in the air."),
(249, 'imbri;', "and now flying he sees the summit and steep sides\nof hardy Atlas, who with his crown props up the sky,\nAtlas, girt constantly, to whom, with black clouds,\nthe pine-bearing head both with wind is beaten and with rain;", "And now, in flight, he sees the summit and the steep flanks of tough Atlas, who props up the sky with his crown; Atlas, whose pine-bearing head, ringed always with black clouds, is lashed by wind and rain;", "Atlas is both a mountain in north-west Africa and the Titan who was made to hold up the sky; here the mountain is an old man with shoulders, chin and beard, and the two are blended. 'Atlantis' is the Greek-style genitive of 'Atlas'; the name is repeated at the head of the next line. 'volans' is a present participle, 'in flight'. 'latera ardua' and 'apicem' are the objects of 'cernit'. 'qui… fulcit' means that he props up the sky 'with his crown (vertice)'. 'cinctum' is a perfect participle agreeing with 'caput', 'girt about'. 'cui' is a dative of possession ('whose head'). 'et vento… et imbri' is a double 'et… et', 'both by wind and by rain', with ablatives of means. Atlas is also the father of Maia, Mercury's mother, as line 258 will recall."),
(250, 'tegit;', "snow poured over covers his shoulders;", "snow, poured down over his shoulders, covers them;", "'infusa' is a perfect participle agreeing with 'nix', 'poured over'. 'umeros' can be read as the object of 'tegit' ('the snow covers his shoulders') or, with 'infusa', as a Greek-style accusative of the part ('poured over as to the shoulders'); the sense is the same either way. The man-mountain is dressed in snow."),
(251, 'barba.', "then rivers from the chin\nplunge of the old man, and with ice stiffens the bristling beard.", "then streams tumble from the old man's chin, and ice sets his shaggy beard stiff.", "'flumina' is the subject of 'praecipitant', which is used intransitively here, 'plunge down'. 'mento' is an ablative of separation, 'from the chin'; 'senis' (a genitive, 'of the old man') is held back to the end of its line. 'glacie' is an ablative of means, and 'horrida' ('bristling, shaggy') agrees with 'barba'. 'riget' means 'is stiff'."),
(253, 'constitit;', "Here first, resting on matched wings, the Cyllenian\nhalted;", "Here first Mercury paused, poised on level wings;", "'Hic' is the adverb 'here', and not the pronoun. 'Cyllenius' is Mercury, named for Mount Cyllene in Arcadia, where he was born. 'paribus… alis' is an ablative of means, 'on balanced wings'. 'nitens' is the present participle of the deponent 'nitor', 'resting, balancing'. 'constitit' (from 'consisto') is 'came to a stop'."),
(255, 'iuxta.', "from there headlong with his whole body to the waves\nhe sent himself, like a bird, which around the shores, around\nthe fish-rich rocks flies low, along the waters.", "then from there he flung himself headlong, with his whole body, down toward the waves, like a bird that flies low around the shores, around the rocks where fish abound, close to the water.", "'praeceps' is a predicative adjective, 'headlong'; 'toto corpore' is an ablative of manner. 'se… misit' is 'sent himself'. 'avi similis' — 'avi' is a dative of 'avis' with 'similis' (which also takes the genitive); the simile is a seabird. 'quae' is the relative, 'which', its antecedent 'avi'. 'litora circum' has the preposition after its noun, and 'circum' is repeated before 'piscosos scopulos' ('fish-rich rocks'). 'humilis' is a nominative adjective used like an adverb, 'low', agreeing with 'quae'. 'aequora iuxta' is anastrophe again: 'iuxta' takes the accusative, 'beside the waters'."),
(258, 'proles.', "Not otherwise between the lands and the sky he was flying,\nthe sandy shore of Libya and the winds he was cutting,\nfrom his maternal grandfather coming, the Cyllenian offspring.", "Just so, between earth and sky, he flew, cutting his way along the sandy shore of Libya and through the winds: the Cyllenian son, coming from his mother's father.", "'terras inter caelumque' is 'inter terras caelumque', with 'inter' placed after the first noun. 'volabat… secabat' are imperfects, 'was flying… was cutting'; 'litus harenosum Libyae ventosque' is the double object of 'secabat', 'cut through'. 'Cyllenia proles' is Mercury (the nominative 'proles', 'offspring'), and he is the subject, which is held to the end of the sentence. 'materno ab avo' means 'from his mother's father', Atlas: Mercury's mother Maia was Atlas's daughter. The Latin Library prints an extra 'ad' in 257, which changes nothing of substance."),
(261, 'conspicit;', "As soon as with winged soles he touched the huts,\nAeneas founding citadels and renewing buildings\nhe sees;", "As soon as he touches the huts of Carthage with his winged feet, he sees Aeneas laying the foundations of citadels and building new houses;", "'Ut primum' with the perfect indicative 'tetigit' means 'as soon as he touched'. 'alatis… plantis' is an ablative of means, 'with winged soles'. 'magalia' (neuter plural) are huts or cottages, a word of Punic origin. 'fundantem' and 'novantem' are present participles agreeing with 'Aenean' (Greek accusative; you'll see this constantly), the object of 'conspicit'; 'arces' and 'tecta' are their objects. The irony is that Aeneas is founding a city, but it is not his city."),
(264, 'auro.', "and to him starred with tawny jasper\na sword was, and with Tyrian purple glowed the cloak\nhanging from his shoulders, which, gifts, rich Dido\nhad made, and with fine gold had picked out the webs.", "At his side was a sword studded with tawny jasper, and a cloak glowed with Tyrian purple where it hung from his shoulders: a gift that rich Dido had made, picking out the weave with slender gold.", "'illi… ensis erat' is a dative of possession, 'he had a sword'. 'stellatus iaspide fulva' means 'starred (studded) with yellow jasper', the ablative of means. 'Tyrio… murice' is the ablative of the murex shellfish, the source of Tyrian purple. 'laena demissa' is a perfect participle, 'hanging down'. 'dives quae munera Dido fecerat': 'dives' belongs to Dido ('rich Dido'), not to the gifts. 'quae' is ambiguous: as feminine singular it points back to 'laena' ('which cloak Dido had made as gifts'), as neuter plural it goes with 'munera' ('which gifts Dido had made', in apposition to the cloak). Either way the sense is 'a cloak that rich Dido had made as a gift for him'. 'discreverat' is the pluperfect of 'discerno', here 'had picked out, marked off' the web with thread of gold. Dido's gift is the outfit of her new husband, and this is how Mercury finds him."),
(265, 'invadit:', "At once he attacks:", "At once he accosts him:", "'Continuo' is an adverb, 'at once'. 'invadit' is 'goes at, attacks', and it is used of words, with Aeneas as an unexpressed object."),
(267, 'tuarum?', "“You now of lofty Carthage the foundations\nlay, and a beautiful city, wife-ridden,\nyou build up, alas, forgetful of your kingdom and your own affairs?", "“Are you now laying the foundations of lofty Carthage, and raising a fine city like a husband ruled by his wife, forgetful, alas, of your own kingdom and your own affairs?", "This opens Mercury's speech, so Greenough prints the opening quotation mark; it runs to 276. The question has no interrogative word: it rests on tone, and on 'heu'. 'locas' means 'you place, lay'. 'uxorius' is a nominative adjective, 'devoted to a wife, under her thumb'; it is the first sneer. 'pulchram… urbem' encloses it. 'exstruis' means 'build up'. 'oblite' is a vocative singular, the perfect participle of the deponent 'obliviscor', which takes the genitive ('regni rerumque… tuarum'). The 'rerumque oblite' has an elision (-que before o-). 'Karthaginis altae' is 'of lofty Carthage', and the epithet makes the city sound grander than a few huts."),
(269, 'torquet;', "Himself of the gods to you me from bright Olympus sends\nthe ruler, who heaven and lands with his will turns;", "The ruler of the gods himself sends me down to you from bright Olympus, he who sways heaven and earth by his will;", "'Ipse… regnator' is the subject, split apart by the rest of the clause; 'ipse' is emphatic, 'in his own person', and is repeated in 270. 'deum' is the old genitive plural for 'deorum' (you'll see this constantly: 'divom', 'Danaum'). 'claro… Olympo' is an ablative of separation, 'from bright Olympus', without a preposition. 'numine' is an ablative of means, 'by his divine will'. 'torquet' means 'turns, swings', which is to say governs."),
(270, 'auras:', "he himself bids me to carry these commands through the swift breezes:", "he himself orders me to carry this charge through the swift air:", "'ferre iubet' takes 'me' (from the line before) as its understood accusative subject. 'haec… mandata' is a neuter plural phrase, framing 'ferre iubet celeris'. 'celeris… auras' is an accusative plural in '-is'. Mercury repeats Jupiter's instructions at 226 almost word for word ('celeris defer mea dicta per auras'), as messengers in epic often do; the repetition, and the repeated 'ipse', give the speech its weight."),
(271, 'terris?', "what do you build, or with what hope in Libyan lands do you wear away your leisure?", "What are you contriving, and with what hope do you waste your leisure on Libyan soil?", "This echoes 235 ('quid struit, aut qua spe…'), but the third person ('struit') becomes the second ('struis'). 'teris otia' is 'you rub away, wear out your idle time'; 'otia' is the neuter plural, 'leisure' as a fault. 'Libycis… terris' is an ablative of place without a preposition. 'qua spe' is an ablative of attendant circumstance."),
(276, 'debentur.', "If no glory of such great things moves you,\n[nor yourself, over your own praise, do you work at the toil,]\nAscanius rising and the hopes of the heir Iulus,\nregard, to whom the kingdom of Italy and the Roman land\nare owed.”", "If the glory of such great enterprises does not move you, [and you do not yourself undertake the toil for your own renown,] think of Ascanius as he grows up, and of the hopes that rest on Iulus, your heir, to whom the kingdom of Italy and the land of Rome are owed.”", "Line 273, '[nec super ipse tua moliris laude laborem,]', is printed in square brackets because Greenough marks it as spurious: it is Jupiter's line 233 again, with 'sua' changed to 'tua' and 'molitur' to 'moliris'. We keep it, as the edition does; the sentence also reads on without it, from 272 straight to 274. 'te' is the object of 'movet', and 'nulla… gloria' is the subject. 'respice' is the imperative of 'respicio', 'have regard for', and the apodosis of the 'si' clause. 'Ascanium surgentem' means 'Ascanius as he rises (grows up)'; 'spes… heredis Iuli' means 'the hopes of your heir Iulus', 'Iulus' being Ascanius under his other name. 'cui' is a dative of the person to whom, and 'regnum Italiae Romanaque tellus' are its two subjects, with the plural verb 'debentur' ('are owed'); the Latin Library has the singular 'debetur', which only changes the number. Greenough prints the closing quotation mark after the full stop, with a space, which is how the text reads here; it closes Mercury's speech."),
(278, 'auram.', "With such a mouth the Cyllenian, having spoken,\nmortal sights in the middle of the talk left,\nand far off into thin air from the eyes vanished.", "When the Cyllenian had spoken with such a voice, he left mortal sight in the middle of his speech, and far off, from before his eyes, melted into the thin air.", "'Tali… ore locutus' — 'locutus' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'loquor', with active sense, 'having spoken'; 'ore' is an ablative of means, 'with such a voice'. 'mortalis visus' is accusative plural, 'mortal sights', that is, mortal eyes: 'mortalis' is the '-is' accusative plural, agreeing with 'visus' (the fourth-declension noun). 'medio sermone' is an ablative, 'in the middle of the talk'. 'evanuit' is the perfect of 'evanesco', 'vanished'. 'in tenuem… auram' means 'into thin air', and 'procul' and 'ex oculis' mark how complete the disappearance is. A god comes in disguise and leaves at will."),
(280, 'haesit.', "But truly Aeneas at the sight was struck dumb, out of his mind,\nand his hair stood on end with horror, and his voice stuck in his throat.", "But Aeneas, at the sight, was struck dumb and out of his wits; his hair stood stiff with horror, and his voice stuck in his throat.", "'At vero' marks the change to Aeneas. 'obmutuit' is the perfect of 'obmutesco', 'was struck dumb'; 'amens' is the nominative adjective, 'out of his mind'; 'aspectu' is an ablative of cause. 'arrectae… comae' is a perfect participle in the nominative plural agreeing with 'comae', with 'sunt' left out: 'his hair was raised'. 'horrore' is an ablative of cause, and 'faucibus' an ablative of place. 'haesit' is the perfect of 'haereo', 'stuck'."),
(282, 'deorum.', "He burns to depart in flight and to leave the sweet lands,\nstunned by so great a warning and command of the gods.", "He burns to go, to flee and leave the pleasant land behind, stunned by such a warning, and by such a command of the gods.", "'Ardet' with two infinitives, 'abire' and 'relinquere', is a poetic construction, 'burns to go'. 'fuga' is an ablative of manner, 'in flight'. 'dulcisque… terras' is framed round 'relinquere', with 'dulcis' the accusative plural in '-is'. 'attonitus' is the perfect participle of 'attono', 'thunderstruck', agreeing with Aeneas. 'tanto monitu imperioque' are ablatives of cause, and 'deorum' is the genitive plural; the single 'tanto' goes with both nouns."),
(283, 'agat?', "Alas, what is he to do?", "Alas, what is he to do?", "'agat' is a deliberative subjunctive, 'what is he to do?'. The poet puts Aeneas's questions to himself in the third person, which is how the passage makes us listen in. The three subjunctives of 283–284 ('agat', 'audeat', 'sumat') are all of this kind."),
(284, 'adfatu?', "With what, now, the queen to approach, raging,\nis he to dare with an address?", "With what words is he to dare to approach the frantic queen now?", "'quo… adfatu' is 'with what address (word of address)?', split by the hyperbaton; 'adfatu' is an ablative of means. 'ambire' means 'go round, approach, canvass'. 'audeat' is a deliberative subjunctive, taking the infinitive 'ambire'. 'furentem' is a present participle, 'raging', 'frantic' with love, anticipating what he fears. 'reginam' and 'furentem' enclose 'ambire'."),
(284, 'sumat?', "What first beginnings is he to take?", "How is he to begin?", "'exordia' is a neuter plural, 'beginnings, openings', and is the object of 'sumat', the deliberative subjunctive again. 'prima' is its adjective, 'first'."),
(286, 'versat.', "And his swift mind now hither, now thither he divides,\nand into various parts he snatches it and through all things turns it.", "He divides his quick mind now this way, now that, drags it off in different directions and turns it over through every course.", "'animum… celerem' is a hyperbaton: 'celerem' agrees with 'animum', which is the object of all three verbs, 'dividit', 'rapit', 'versat'. 'nunc huc… nunc illuc' is 'now here, now there'. 'in partisque… perque omnia' is a double '-que… -que' (you'll see this constantly), with 'partis' the accusative plural in '-is'. 'rapit' and 'versat' (frequentative, 'keeps turning') show the mind being pulled about. The line is a picture of an agitated mind."),
(287, 'visa est:', "To him wavering, this better plan seemed:", "To him, in his wavering, this plan seemed the better:", "'alternanti' is a dative present participle, 'to him as he wavered', the dative of the person to whom something seems, with 'visa est' (the perfect passive of 'video' used as 'seemed'). 'potior sententia' is the comparative, 'the better plan'. The colon announces the plan, which follows in indirect form."),
(291, 'dissimulent;', "Mnestheus and Sergestus he calls, and brave Serestus,\nthat they fit out the fleet, silent, and gather their comrades to the shore,\nthat they prepare arms, and what the cause of the changing of affairs\nmay be, conceal;", "He summons Mnestheus, Sergestus and brave Serestus: they are to fit out the fleet in silence, gather their men at the shore, make ready their arms, and hide the reason for the change of plan;", "Mnestheus, Sergestus and Serestus are Trojan captains. 'Mnesthea' is the Greek accusative (like 'Aenean'), and the other two are ordinary accusatives, all objects of 'vocat'. 'aptent… cogant… parent… dissimulent' are subjunctives of command with 'ut' left out after 'vocat' (the order is given in the plain subjunctive, 'let them fit out'). 'taciti' is a nominative adjective used like an adverb, 'in silence'. 'quae… sit causa' is an indirect question, hence 'sit', the subject of the question being 'causa'; 'rebus novandis' is a dative of the gerundive, 'for the changing of the situation'. 'et' links 'parent' and 'dissimulent'."),
(294, 'modus.', "that he himself meanwhile, since the best Dido\nknows not and does not hope that so great a love can be broken,\nwill try the approaches, and what the softest of speaking\ntimes, what way favourable for the circumstances.", "and that he himself, meanwhile, will look for ways of approach: since the excellent Dido knows nothing and does not expect so great a love to be broken, he will seek the gentlest moment for speaking and the right way to go about it.", "The order passes into an accusative and infinitive: 'sese… temptaturum (esse)', with 'sese' an emphatic 'se', 'that he himself will try'. 'aditus' is the accusative plural of 'aditus', 'approaches'. 'quando… nesciat… speret' is a causal clause with subjunctives (it is Aeneas's reasoning, 'since, as he thinks'); 'optuma' is Greenough's spelling of 'optima', 'excellent'; 'tantos rumpi… amores' is an accusative and passive infinitive after 'speret', and 'amores' is the poetic plural. 'quae mollissima fandi tempora, quis rebus dexter modus' are indirect questions with the verbs ('sint', 'sit') left out; 'fandi' is the genitive of the gerund of 'for', 'of speaking'; 'quis' is the old form of 'quibus' and goes with 'rebus': 'quis rebus dexter modus' is 'what way is favourable in (or for) the circumstances', and the sense is the same whether 'rebus' is felt as dative or ablative. 'dexter' means 'favourable', literally 'on the right hand'."),
(295, 'facessunt.', "More swiftly all\nglad at the command obey and carry out the orders.", "All of them obey the order gladly and with greater speed, and carry out their instructions.", "'Ocius' is a comparative adverb, 'more quickly' (than might be expected). 'imperio' can go with 'laeti' (an ablative of cause, 'glad at the order') or with 'parent' ('pareo' takes the dative: 'obey the order'), and it may do both jobs at once; the literal version takes it with 'laeti', the free version with 'parent'. 'iussa' is a perfect participle used as a noun, 'the orders'. 'facessunt' (from 'facesso') means 'carry out, perform'."),
(296, 'amantem?', "But the queen the wiles — who could deceive a lover?", "But the queen — the plot — who could ever deceive one in love?", "'At' marks the contrast with the men's obedience. 'dolos' is the object of 'praesensit', held back to 297; the clause is broken off by a dash, which Greenough prints with no space ('dolos—quis'), and the poet interjects a question. It cannot be cut at the dash, so the whole stretch is one unit. 'possit' is a potential subjunctive, 'who could?', in a rhetorical question; 'amantem' is a present participle used as a noun, 'one who loves', object of 'fallere'. This begins a paragraph."),
(298, 'timens.', "sensed beforehand, and the movements first caught, those to come,\nfearing all things safe.", "she sensed it beforehand, and was the first to catch the stirrings of what was to come, afraid of everything, even what was safe.", "The verb, 'praesensit', completes the sentence begun at 296 with 'dolos'. 'excepit' means 'caught, picked up' (the perfect of 'excipio', with a long e). 'motus… futuros' is the accusative plural of 'motus' with the future participle of 'sum', 'movements about to be', that is, what is coming. 'prima' is a nominative adjective used like an adverb, 'the first to'. 'omnia tuta timens' is a present participle phrase, 'fearing all things safe', 'afraid even of what was safe'. The participle 'timens' agrees with 'regina'."),
(299, 'parari.', "The same wicked Rumour to her raging\nreported the fleet to be armed and the voyage to be prepared.", "That same wicked Rumour brought word to her in her frenzy that the fleet was being armed and the voyage made ready.", "'Eadem… Fama' is the Rumour of 173–197, who here comes back, as Iarbas's prayer has shown how she works. 'impia' means 'wicked', an offence against 'pietas'. 'furenti' is a dative present participle, 'to her raging'. 'detulit' is the perfect of 'defero', 'reported, brought word'. 'armari… parari' are passive infinitives in an accusative and infinitive after 'detulit', with 'classem' and 'cursum' as their subjects."),
(303, 'Cithaeron.', "She rages, lacking in mind, and, inflamed, through the whole city\nshe revels, like, roused by the stirred sacred rites,\na Thyiad, when, Bacchus having been heard, they goad, the triennial\norgies, and night-time Cithaeron calls with shouting.", "She rages, robbed of her reason, and burns through the whole city in a frenzy, like a Thyiad roused by the shaken sacred emblems, when the cry of Bacchus is heard, and the revels of the festival that comes every other year goad her on, and Cithaeron calls to her by night with shouting.", "'inops animi' is 'lacking in mind', with the genitive of respect. 'incensa' is a perfect participle, 'inflamed', and 'totamque… per urbem' has 'per' set after its adjective and 'que'. 'bacchatur' is the deponent 'bacchor', 'revels like a Bacchant, rages'. The simile: 'qualis… Thyias' (the verb is left out) is 'like a Thyiad', a Greek nominative, a woman worshipper of Bacchus. 'commotis… sacris' is an ablative absolute: the sacred objects are carried or shaken in the procession. 'excita' is a perfect participle agreeing with 'Thyias'. 'audito… Baccho' is an ablative absolute: 'Bacchus' is the god's name used for his cry. 'stimulant' has the Thyiad as an understood object, and 'orgia' (neuter plural, 'rites') as subject, with 'trieterica' agreeing with it. 'trieterica' is Greek for 'every third year', counting both ends the Greek way, which is to say every other year: the literal version says 'triennial', the free version gives the real interval. 'Cithaeron' is the mountain of Bacchic rites near Thebes, 'night-time' because the rites were held by night. The simile makes Dido a woman possessed."),
(304, 'ultro:', "At last with these words Aeneas she addresses of her own accord:", "At last she speaks first to Aeneas, in these words:", "'compellat' is 'accosts, addresses'. 'ultro' means 'of her own accord, unasked', before Aeneas has found the courage: she takes the first step, so the plan to wait for the right moment fails at once. 'Aenean' is the Greek accusative. The speech follows in the next part."),
]
